<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) Ch. VII — tissue cells as hexagons / froth vs. human corneal endothelium (Pass 2: segmented specimen, sweep, controls, statistics) (Homo sapiens — corneal endothelium in situ (ocular monolayer epithelium); Elodea canadensis leaf epidermis (secondary, visual only))",
        "Thompson (1917) Ch. VII — tissue cells as hexagons / froth vs. human corneal endothelium (Pass 2: segmented specimen, sweep, controls, statistics) faithfully reproduces Homo sapiens — corneal endothelium in situ (ocular monolayer epithelium); Elodea canadensis leaf epidermis (secondary, visual only) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-019-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-019-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-019-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-019-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-019-5>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-019-6> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) thompson/claims/t1917-019_the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-aggregates.py, morphoeval/lean/loops/Thompson1917_T1917_019.lean, morphoeval/math_kg_loops/thompson1917-t1917-019.yaml implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Homo sapiens — corneal endothelium in situ (ocular monolayer epithelium); Elodea canadensis leaf epidermis (secondary, visual only) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/morphoeval/lean/loops/Thompson1917_T1917_019.lean>,
        <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/morphoeval/math_kg_loops/thompson1917-t1917-019.yaml>,
        <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-019_the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-aggregates.py> ;
    schema:about "Homo sapiens — corneal endothelium in situ (ocular monolayer epithelium); Elodea canadensis leaf epidermis (secondary, visual only)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: No um/px scale, so no cell density or absolute area is reported",
        "does_not_claim: The Elodea canadensis image (Thompson Fig. 126 species) is vendored for visual comparison only; chloroplast clutter defeats the automatic pipeline",
        "does_not_claim: The proliferating-epithelium qualification rests on Gibson 2006's published spectrum, not on a segmented proliferating specimen",
        "does_not_claim: n = 1 specimen: no population statement about human corneal endothelia",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Interior-cell polygon statistics, Crofton perimeters, 2x2-window junction valence, Lewis and Aboav-Weaire fits, bootstrap CIs",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Mean-field Gibson 2006 division-topology Markov chain with through-centre cleavage (reconstruction; uniform-random cleavage ablation)",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Nucleus-marker watershed segmentation of the alizarin-red corneal endothelium; distance-peak watershed for line drawings",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Periodic Voronoi froth morphospace with Gaussian positional disorder delta and Lloyd relaxation; Poisson-Voronoi null",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Rasterised-froth pipeline control: pixel perimeter bias (-0.9 %) and finite-window <n> bias (-0.18) measured on a tessellation with known exact statistics",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson Fig. 125 (after Rhumbler): soap froth between plates, compressed (A) and relaxed (B)",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson p. 305: cellular tissue resembles a froth",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson p. 309/323: partitions meet three at a point at ~120 degrees (Lamarle minimal-area condition)",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson p. 321: hexagonal pattern illustrated in countless cases but perfectly regular only rarely; eye pigment epithelium as the exemplar",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson p. 325: cells on the average hexagonal, some with fewer and some with more sides than six",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson p. 330: closely packed hexagons give the minimal extent of boundary in a plane",
        "transfer_boundary: Clinical norms (Sheng & Bullimore 2007) are specular-microscopy regressions for living corneas; the specimen is an ex vivo alizarin-red flat mount",
        "transfer_boundary: Lloyd (centroidal Voronoi) relaxation is a proxy for surface-tension perimeter minimisation, not a Plateau / vertex-model energy descent",
        "transfer_boundary: The honeycomb bound is applied cell-by-cell to the measured area distribution (Hales 2001 proves the equal-area case); the measured excess includes real wall waviness as well as polygon irregularity" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-019_the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-aggregates.py, https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/morphoeval/lean/loops/Thompson1917_T1917_019.lean, https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/morphoeval/math_kg_loops/thompson1917-t1917-019.yaml; output: docs/media/t1917_019_the_forms_of_tissues_or_cell_a_comparison.png; organism: Homo sapiens — corneal endothelium in situ (ocular monolayer epithelium); Elodea canadensis leaf epidermis (secondary, visual only); environment: n/a; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Thompson p. 305: cellular tissue resembles a froth | Thompson p. 309/323: partitions meet three at a point at ~120 degrees (Lamarle minimal-area condition) | Thompson p. 321: hexagonal pattern illustrated in countless cases but perfectly regular only rarely; eye pigment epithelium as the exemplar | Thompson p. 325: cells on the average hexagonal, some with fewer and some with more sides than six | Thompson p. 330: closely packed hexagons give the minimal extent of boundary in a plane | Thompson Fig. 125 (after Rhumbler): soap froth between plates, compressed (A) and relaxed (B); reconstructed_in_script: Periodic Voronoi froth morphospace with Gaussian positional disorder delta and Lloyd relaxation; Poisson-Voronoi null | Nucleus-marker watershed segmentation of the alizarin-red corneal endothelium; distance-peak watershed for line drawings | Interior-cell polygon statistics, Crofton perimeters, 2x2-window junction valence, Lewis and Aboav-Weaire fits, bootstrap CIs | Mean-field Gibson 2006 division-topology Markov chain with through-centre cleavage (reconstruction; uniform-random cleavage ablation) | Rasterised-froth pipeline control: pixel perimeter bias (-0.9 %) and finite-window <n> bias (-0.18) measured on a tessellation with known exact statistics; transfer_boundary: Lloyd (centroidal Voronoi) relaxation is a proxy for surface-tension perimeter minimisation, not a Plateau / vertex-model energy descent | The honeycomb bound is applied cell-by-cell to the measured area distribution (Hales 2001 proves the equal-area case); the measured excess includes real wall waviness as well as polygon irregularity | Clinical norms (Sheng & Bullimore 2007) are specular-microscopy regressions for living corneas; the specimen is an ex vivo alizarin-red flat mount; does_not_claim: n = 1 specimen: no population statement about human corneal endothelia | No um/px scale, so no cell density or absolute area is reported | The proliferating-epithelium qualification rests on Gibson 2006's published spectrum, not on a segmented proliferating specimen | The Elodea canadensis image (Thompson Fig. 126 species) is vendored for visual comparison only; chloroplast clutter defeats the automatic pipeline]; other sources: On Growth and Form — Ch. VII The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (pp. 293-345, Figs. 120-127) <https://archive.org/details/ongrowthform1917thom>, Morphology of human corneal endothelium in situ (alizarin red) — Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0 <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morphology_of_human_corneal_endothelium_in_situ.jpg>; phenomenon_size: 979 µm (0.0009792 m, log10=-3.009 m, tier=5_primordium_100um); species_scale_ladder (20 runs from 979 µm to 2.85 m): 979 µm:cortical-sulcal-axon-tension, 1.4 mm:bressloff-cowan-V1-hallucination, 1.5 mm:corticothalamic-seizure-spirals, 1.7 mm:homo-sapiens-bioelectric-polarity, 3.1 mm:vertex-glass-jamming, 3.4 mm:homo-sapiens-vertex-packing, 6.3 cm:homo-sapiens-turing-wavelength, 11.7 cm:homo-sapiens-clock-wavefront" ;
    pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.1090380305>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1007/s004540010071>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0026-0800(70)90038-8>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2007.11.049>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature05014>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1080/00107518408210979>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1097/ICO.0b013e318033a6da>,
        <https://doi.org/10.15252/embj.201592374> ;
    pk:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-code-traceability>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-model-fidelity>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-morphometric-validation>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-overall>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-parameter-expressivity>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-repro-package>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-source-traceability>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-019-specimen-match> ;
    pk:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-10>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-11>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-12>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-13>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-14>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-15>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-16>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-019-the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-a-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-019-2>,
